      "title": "Australian politics live: net overseas migration falls before Labor migration cuts; Hanson scolds reporter over Payman question",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2026/sep/17/labor-immigration-policy-tony-burke-anthony-albanese-one-nation-ntwnfb",
      "description": "<p>Follow the day’s news live</p><ul><li><p>Get our <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=cvau_sfl\">breaking news email</a>, <a href=\"https://app.adjust.com/w4u7jx3\">free app</a> or <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/series/full-story?CMP=cvau_sfl\">daily news podcast</a></p></li></ul><p><strong>Barnaby Joyce</strong> says Labor is copying and pasting One Nation’s homework because the measures we know so far around blocking the families of international students and preventing visa hopping have some similarity to what <strong>Pauline Hanson</strong> is suggesting.</p><p>Speaking to the Today show a little earlier, Joyce says Burke’s last minute cancellation at the press club was “the greatest no-show since Amelia Earhart”.</p><p>[Burke] can’t possibly be about to announce One Nation’s policy. I mean, Tony’s a great guy, right? I like Tony, but honestly, he was the greatest no-show since Amelia Earhart when he didn’t turn up to the Press Club …</p><p>What I’ve heard thus far is that it’s an exact replication, but not to the extent of what One Nation is doing.</p><p>I will be watching this with absolute fascination and some trepidation, and I’ll be waiting for Jim Chalmers to explain his new budget.</p><p>I was on polling booths with my colleagues like <strong>Sally Sitou</strong>. I was out there in Reid with her in those last weeks of the campaign, standing there for hours at a time. She would be surrounded by men a foot taller than her, stopping her physically talking to or giving information to voters.</p><p>I would think secretly paying known rightwing figures to disrupt events and pretend that they are just disgruntled citizens having their say is certainly immoral, if it’s not illegal.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2026/sep/17/labor-immigration-policy-tony-burke-anthony-albanese-one-nation-ntwnfb\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-09-17T02:04:21"
    },
    {
      "title": "Labor backs down on veterans’ health services cap after furious backlash from Jacqui Lambie and ex-ADF members",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/sep/17/veterans-allied-health-care-cap-jacqui-lambie-labor-backdown",
      "description": "<p>Independent senator had denounced as ‘cruel’ the plan to impose a $5,000 cap on allied health services for veterans</p><ul><li><p><a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2026/sep/17/labor-immigration-policy-tony-burke-anthony-albanese-one-nation-ntwnfb\">Follow our Australia news live blog for latest updates</a></p></li><li><p>Get our <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=cvau_sfl\">breaking news email</a>, <a href=\"https://app.adjust.com/w4u7jx3\">free app</a> or <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/series/full-story?CMP=cvau_sfl\">daily news podcast</a></p></li></ul><p>Labor has backed down on plans to impose a $5,000 cap on health services for defence veterans, caving to growing political pressure after weeks of criticism.</p><p>The government announced it would no longer pursue the cap for allied health services, which had been to come into force on 1 July 2027, after a weeks-long campaign by the opposition, veterans’ groups and independent senator Jacqui Lambie.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/sep/17/veterans-allied-health-care-cap-jacqui-lambie-labor-backdown\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-09-17T00:52:40"
    },
    {
      "title": "Teenage boys charged with murder after alleged mistaken identity shooting of Sydney student",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/sep/17/teenage-boys-charged-with-murder-university-student-ntwnfb",
      "description": "<p>Boys aged 14 and 16 charged after Marco Tapia, 23, was killed inside his family’s home in Glen Alpine</p><ul><li><p><a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2026/sep/17/labor-immigration-policy-tony-burke-anthony-albanese-one-nation-ntwnfb\">Follow our Australia news live blog for latest updates</a></p></li><li><p>Get our <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=cvau_sfl\">breaking news email</a>, <a href=\"https://app.adjust.com/w4u7jx3\">free app</a> or <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/series/full-story?CMP=cvau_sfl\">daily news podcast</a></p></li></ul><p>Two teenage boys have been charged with murder after the shooting death of a university student in Sydney this month, which police say was a case of mistaken identity.</p><p>Marco Tapia, 23, was shot inside his family’s home in Glen Alpine early on 1 September.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/sep/17/teenage-boys-charged-with-murder-university-student-ntwnfb\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-09-17T00:42:05"
